{
  // Declaration emit for the distributable package. `tsconfig.json` is the
  // *checking* config (`noEmit`, and it includes `test/`); this one only writes
  // `dist/types/**`. The JavaScript comes from esbuild — see `build:js` — so
  // nothing here emits `.js`.
  //
  // ## Why `src/server` took a source change to get in here
  //
  // Declaration emit for it used to fail outright, and the failure mode is worth
  // keeping written down because it is silent:
  //
  //   src/server/story-do.ts(173,17): error TS4094: Property 'sql' of exported
  //     anonymous class type may not be private or protected.
  //
  // `createStoryDO` / `createSpaceDO` each `return class ... extends
  // DurableObject` — a class expression tsc cannot name — so the emitter has to
  // serialise the type inline, and it refuses to serialise one carrying a
  // private or protected member. It then writes **no file at all** rather than
  // an incomplete one: `story-do.d.ts` and `space-do.d.ts` were simply absent,
  // and `server/types.d.ts` dangled on `DurableObjectNamespace<StoryDO>`.
  // `--noCheck` does not help; the diagnostic comes from the emitter, not the
  // checker.
  //
  // **The `#private` half of the old fix note was wrong.** Converting all
  // thirteen members to ECMAScript private changes nothing — tsc reports
  // `Property '#sql' ...` in the same place, and reports `ctx` and `env` too,
  // which are `protected` on `DurableObject` itself and are not ours to rename.
  // A class expression extending `DurableObject` can never be serialised
  // inline, whatever we do to our own members.
  //
  // So the fix is the other one: both factories declare their return type, and
  // `StoryDOInstance` / `SpaceDOInstance` are the names the emitter writes. Each
  // interface header says why it exists.
  //
  // ## Why the three `types` conditions moved together
  //
  // Half-and-half does not work, and it fails in the least obvious place.
  // Structurally identical declarations of a generic type are **not**
  // interchangeable: TypeScript's variance fast path only applies to two
  // references to the *same* declaration, so with `folio/core` resolved to
  // `dist/types` and `folio/server` resolved to source,
  //
  //   createFolio({ blocks: [defineBlock({ ... })] })
  //
  // fails with "Type 'BlockDef<{...}>' is not assignable to type 'AnyBlockDef'"
  // — `AnyBlockDef` is `BlockDef<any>`, and across two declarations
  // `strictFunctionTypes` compares `render`'s parameter structurally instead.
  // That is the first call in every host's file, which is why `package.json`
  // moves core, engine and server in one commit or not at all.
  //
  // ## Why `src/admin` and `src/preview` are not in `include`
  //
  // A different and permanent reason: they are entry points to the *host's*
  // client bundle, so they ship as source and are never imported as types. See
  // `package.json`'s `exports` and `src/vite/index.ts`.
  //
  // Note that `dist/types/preview/Render.d.ts` and `RichText.d.ts` are emitted
  // anyway, and correctly: `src/server/index.tsx` re-exports `FolioDoc` from
  // `../preview/Render`, so the server's own declarations reference them. Being
  // reachable from an included file is what pulls a declaration in, not being
  // listed here — which is also why `preview/mount.tsx`, reachable from nothing
  // on this side, stays out.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": false,
    "emitDeclarationOnly": true,
    "declaration": true,
    // Points at the `src/` this package ships, so jump-to-definition from a
    // consumer's editor lands on real TypeScript rather than the declaration.
    "declarationMap": true,
    "rootDir": "src",
    "outDir": "dist/types"
  },
  "include": ["src/core", "src/server", "src/vite"]
}
